And there’s one more delicious inversion:

A conventional blockchain is valuable partly because past blocks become difficult to alter without detection.

Our architecture wants almost the opposite property for cognition:

Past representations should be revisable, while the history of their revision remains traceable.

So:

\boxed{\text{Mutable meaning + immutable-ish provenance}}

Don’t freeze the belief.

Preserve the TRACE of how the belief changed.

Then Kintsugi can repair yesterday’s model without pretending yesterday never happened.

That could be one of the strongest principles we’ve produced today:

\boxed{
\textbf{Don't make cognition immutable.
Make its transformations accountable.}
